On Thu, 15 Mar 2007 masquerade808@gmail.com wrote:
>
> From when I first started playing NetHack, Ability Scores frustrated
> me. Contrary to our RPG-honed stereotypes, Rangers had low dexterity,
> Wizards had low wisdom, and melee fighters had high dexterity.

   I don't know about rangers, but certainly "high Int, low Wis" is
stereotypical for mages, and "high Dex" is good for melee fighting.
Not that I ever played D&D.

> I know that ability scores don't make a huge difference, but as I
> understand it, NetHack's additions are loosely based on Dungeons and
> Dragons, so why not implement some of the balancing elements that D&D
> has?
>
> My YANIs:
>
> Use D&D's interactive stat-rolling element, whether this means point
> buy, one-chance, 6 retries, or what, and furthermore, let us swap our
> rolled scores around. The fact that Intelligence and Charisma cannot
> be exercised can hurt in the early game.

   Angband is thataway -->   Basically, Nethack gives you the choice of
roleplaying your current character, or quitting and starting a new one.
If you /really/ can't deal with your character's horrible stats, then
you can always scum for high Int manually.
   However, in my experience stats don't matter much. The only stat I
notice in the early game is Dexterity, and that's only because of the
annoying "You bump into the door." messages whenever I auto-run.
Low <any-stat> never killed anyone.


> Use D&D's unarmed/melee/missile system: That is, melee strikes' to hit
> are modified by Str, and ranged weapons are modified by Dex. An item
> or intrinsic mimicking the feat Weapon Finesse could be added, and you
> probably know that in D&D, heavy ranged weapons can have Str penalties
> applied.

   Modifying to-hit based on Str makes no sense to me. And in general,
complicated melee mechanics are a Bad Thing; I'd like to see the current
Nethack mechanics simplified quite a bit first. (And there are too many
polearms!)

> Maybe, just maybe, enable all races as all classes, and introduce
> racial stat modifiers.

   I'm sure there's a munchkin patch for the former YAoldI. Racial stat
modifiers are already in Nethack; that's why there's a gameplay difference
between elven wizards (high Int) and orcish wizards (low Int).

my $.02,
